    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d698">

                <pb facs="0795" n="785" sameAs="frus:frus1943China/pg785"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">151.10/1980a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-10-27T22:00:00-04:00">October 27,
                            1943—10 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1536. On October 21 House of Representatives by voice vote passed legislation
                    permitting immigration of Chinese into the United States on a quota basis and
                    allowing Chinese legally admitted to the United States for permanent residence
                    to become American citizens. See <pb facs="0796" n="786" xml:id="pg786"/>
                    Department’s 1468, October 12, 5 p.m.<anchor n="25" xml:id="d698-f1"/> Prospects
                    appear good for early action in the Senate.<anchor n="26" xml:id="d698-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d698-f1" n="25" xml:id="d698-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d698-f2" n="26" xml:id="d698-f2-content">
                <p>On November 26, the Senate passed by voice vote the act relating to immigration
                    of Chinese into the United States.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
